WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.288
Tara Smith: In some cases,
people do infect themselves.

00:00:11.288 --> 00:00:12.460
Hi, I'm Tara Smith.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.070
I'm a professor of epidemiology

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.290
at Kent State University in Ohio.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.088
I study infectious diseases

00:00:18.088 --> 00:00:21.270
and have long been interested
in zombie infections.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.520
So, today, we'll be talking
about zombies in movies

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.411
and rating them for realism.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.850
Edwin: It invades the
brain like meningitis.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:34.950
The adrenal glands hemorrhage.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.457
The brain goes into shutdown.

00:00:36.457 --> 00:00:38.707
Two hours, one minute.

00:00:38.707 --> 00:00:41.090
Smith: So, this one is mixed.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.730
So, it does have an incubation period.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.270
It usually takes several days

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.430
between the time that
one is exposed to a virus

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:47.770
and one develops symptoms.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.318
Lori: It restarts the brain?

00:00:49.318 --> 00:00:51.120
Edwin: No, just the brain stem.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.480
Smith: Viruses can get into the brain.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.826
He mentions meningitis,
which is actually about

00:00:54.826 --> 00:00:56.440
kind of the outside covering of the brain,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.840
and then you would say encephalitis

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.440
when it actually goes into
the deeper parts of the brain.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.970
So that can happen. There are viruses

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:04.040
that cause those type of illnesses.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.770
But, you know, once
you're dead, you're dead.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.950
And even if this
reanimated the brain stem,

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.470
that still wouldn't quite make sense

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.380
because you wouldn't be able to walk.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.350
You could breathe, your heart would beat,

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.025
but you wouldn't be actively going out,

00:01:19.025 --> 00:01:20.108
you know, shambling around.

00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:23.240
But that anyone who died
would also become a zombie,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.850
even if they died in other mechanisms.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.250
So, that's a little less realistic.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.333
I mean, yellow fever is one that

00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:33.617
people can get very
ill, be close to death,

00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:36.300
and then sometimes they
come back and feel fine

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:37.525
and then are dead the next day.

00:01:37.525 --> 00:01:40.630
So it has what we call a biphasic illness.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.030
It has different phases.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.480
And, of course, back in the day,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.450
before people had stethoscopes
and things like that,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.882
if you had somebody who, again,

00:01:47.882 --> 00:01:50.587
was very ill with some infection,

00:01:50.587 --> 00:01:52.620
you listen for a heartbeat
or try to feel for a pulse.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:53.960
It might be very weak.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.350
It might not be detectable
to the average person.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.740
And so there are lots of stories

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.090
from outbreaks of the bubonic plague,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.506
outbreaks of cholera,

00:02:03.506 --> 00:02:06.510
and even also yellow fever
and other types of infections

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.148
where people have been
taken out of their homes,

00:02:09.148 --> 00:02:11.930
put in the dead cart,

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.070
going off to be hauled to the graveyard,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.740
and they wake up in the cart.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.180
You know, the human body
decomposes rather quickly.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.890
So it starts out really from the inside

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.400
that you have all these
bacteria in your gut,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.450
in your mouth, on your
skin, that, once you die,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.940
you become a big food source for them.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.010
So they're the ones that do a lot

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.900
of the initial decomposing.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.310
And there's nothing about
this particular virus

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.490
that would stop that.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.300
They have these shamblers that
have taken years and years

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.790
that are still walking
around after the outbreak,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.840
and they would all just
be bones by that time.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.340
Something would have eaten them,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.110
whether it would be the
bacteria that they carry

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.810
or other rats or scavengers
or something like that.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:03.190
Yeah, again, it gets a bump
for the incubation period,

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.740
but downgraded for the reanimation,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.023
so maybe a five.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.560
I mean, as a lab scientist,
[laughs] it makes me cringe.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.230
But you could see that the
virus is liquid on the floor,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.790
and it would not be aerosolized like that.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.218
So, there would be maybe
a little bit of a splash

00:03:33.218 --> 00:03:34.883
in the immediate vicinity,

00:03:34.883 --> 00:03:37.808
but there's nothing that is making

00:03:37.808 --> 00:03:39.860
that liquid become airborne.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.860
But he's in the blue suit with
the helmets and everything,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.752
so obviously this is a lab

00:03:44.752 --> 00:03:47.070
that studies things that are dangerous.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.470
You wouldn't have ventilation

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.830
go into the rest of the building.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.820
What you would have would be filters

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.280
in the ventilation system
that have very small pores

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.090
so they can trap even viruses

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.590
from getting out of the building.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.210
And then that ventilation
usually goes out to fresh air,

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.570
so to outside rather
than inside the building.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.770
So even if there was a few viral
particles that made it out,

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.910
they would be diluted
when they made it outside

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.244
to the fresh air and
not a danger to anyone.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.940
That also would completely not happen,

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.360
even in my laboratory.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.083
No food, no drink in the
laboratory, anything at all.

00:04:25.083 --> 00:04:26.491
[dog sniffing]

00:04:28.654 --> 00:04:30.380
[dog barking]

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.890
There are some animals
that have been trained

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:34.720
to detect certain pathogens.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.407
So, not necessarily like how they are in,

00:04:37.407 --> 00:04:39.660
how they would be in the room air here,

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.718
but sometimes dogs,

00:04:41.718 --> 00:04:45.151
or sometimes they've used pouched rats

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:48.170
that can be trained to diagnose people

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.360
who are infected, like, with
tuberculosis, for example.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.960
So they're using more of the chemicals

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.150
that humans put off when they're infected,

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.170
rather than directly smelling, like,

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.160
the tuberculosis bacterium.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.047
Yeah, this is about a one. [laughs]

00:05:04.239 --> 00:05:05.825
Doctor: He's gonna inject himself.

00:05:05.825 --> 00:05:07.741
Segen: We have no idea
which one's gonna work.

00:05:07.741 --> 00:05:10.227
[zombie moaning]
[teeth chattering]

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.520
Smith: So, they had noticed
previously in the movie

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.200
that people who are sick
are avoided by zombies.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.700
So Pitt is trying to inject
himself with something

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.800
to basically make the zombies ignore him

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.990
and believe that he is sick so
they don't want to bite him.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.320
Of course, we're not trying to
hide from predators like this

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.863
when we're ill, but there are examples

00:05:33.863 --> 00:05:37.550
where we can use microbes to
kind of fight off each other.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.390
Back before antibiotics were common

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.790
for treatment of infections,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.480
they actually used injection with malaria,

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.570
a parasite that causes high fevers,

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:51.340
as an attempt to treat
syphilis, a bacterial infection,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.720
and that actually won
a Nobel Prize in 1927.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.133
Today, we don't use it anymore

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:57.840
because we do have antibiotics.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.470
So, what we do use sometimes

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.250
are viruses called bacteriophage

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.480
that can actually kill bacteria

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.810
that may be resistant
to the drugs we have,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.760
but not quite in the way it's
described in "World War Z."

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.650
In some cases, people
do infect themselves.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:24.350
Probably the most famous of
those was Dr. Barry Marshall.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.625
He was an Australian scientist

00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:28.760
who won the Nobel Prize, again,

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.930
for discovering that a bacterium
called Helicobacter pylori

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.440
causes, first, gastric ulcers.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.010
People didn't necessarily believe him,

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:40.550
so he ended up swallowing a mixture

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.480
of Helicobacter pylori himself

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.600
and then treated himself with antibiotics.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.730
Maybe about a three. [laughs]

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:50.220
I think the book was much
better as far as realism,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.960
but the movie took the concept

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.857
and I think twisted it a little bit.

00:06:53.857 --> 00:06:55.540
Scientist: The chimps are infected!

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.796
Activist: Infected with what?

00:06:57.796 --> 00:06:59.046
Scientist: Rage.

00:06:59.046 --> 00:07:00.652
[chimpanzees hollering]

00:07:00.652 --> 00:07:03.619
[men screaming]
[chimps screaming]

00:07:03.619 --> 00:07:04.890
[blood splatters]

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.001
Activist: [screams] Oh, my God!

00:07:07.001 --> 00:07:09.170
[woman gagging]
[man screaming]

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:12.100
Smith: The incubation period
is basically instantaneous.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.640
When blood goes all over,

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.670
that is how some blood-borne
diseases can be transmitted.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:19.330
For example, Ebola is a disease

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:21.750
that is transmitted by exposure to blood

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:22.930
and other body fluids.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:26.170
In this case, potentially
even if people aren't bitten,

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.150
if that blood goes into their
eyes or other mucus membranes,

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.950
they could be infected that way as well.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.740
So that part could happen.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:37.950
But, again, the incubation
period would take days for this.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.430
In animals, at least, rabies
does cause a lot of aggression.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.540
In humans, a little bit less.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.280
It does cause depression.
It does cause active biting,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.980
because in rabies it's transmitted
through saliva, as well.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.770
So, not too surprising
that a virus called rage

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.003
could have those similar
kind of aggressive, biting.

00:07:58.969 --> 00:08:01.540
[man screaming]
[dramatic music]

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:04.570
Smith: Yeah, so some viruses
do cause eye infections,

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.460
so basically a form of conjunctivitis.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.830
In this case, it's infecting usually

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:11.100
a different place of the eye

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.190
that you don't typically see with viruses.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:14.910
Again, we see that with Ebola.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:15.743
We've seen that a little bit

00:08:15.743 --> 00:08:17.410
with the current coronavirus also,

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.610
that some people do seem
to have eye infections

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:20.530
from that as well.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:23.970
So, there are carrier states
for various types of organisms.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.130
You don't see it as often in viruses,

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.560
but you do have things like HIV

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.180
that people can have chronic infection

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.160
and be infected for a long time

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:35.000
and transmit the virus to others.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.100
So, we actually do see that occasionally

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.750
with blood-borne pathogens,
like not only HIV,

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.340
but hepatitis B virus
and hepatitis C virus.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.100
And they do try to incorporate
some of those aspects

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.740
of the biology of the virus.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.363
So I'd say this one's
maybe a six or a seven.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:52.610
Columbus: It's been two
months since patient zero

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:56.010
took a bite of a contaminated
burger at a Gas N' Gulp.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.740
Just two months, and I might be the last

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:59.658
non-cannibal freak in the country.

00:08:59.658 --> 00:09:03.380
Smith: This is another one
that is not a viral infection.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:04.930
When we're talking about mad cow disease,

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.900
that's actually caused by
what, we call it a prion,

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.560
so an infectious protein.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.570
One of the things about those
is they often are ingested.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.330
That's why the big scare
in the UK many years ago,

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.080
some cows infected with this

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.500
may have gotten into the food chain,

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:19.780
people may have eaten it,

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.110
and potentially could
develop disease that way.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:26.070
But that two-month time frame
would not be realistic at all.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:29.200
This is a condition that
takes many years to develop,

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.870
'cause what happens is
that you ingest the prion

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:34.940
and it ends up in your brain eventually.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:38.070
And what it does is that all
of the proteins in your body

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:40.050
have to be folded a certain way, OK?

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.220
So they have, you know, a
long amino acid sequence,

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.077
but that's just the first part.

00:09:45.077 --> 00:09:48.400
Those amino acids have
to not only come together

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:49.900
one after another in the right way,

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:50.960
but also they have to have

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.470
what we call a tertiary structure,

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.540
so a three-dimensional structure

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.730
that has to be a certain way

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:59.290
in order for the protein
to function correctly.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:00.880
And so what this prion does

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.030
is that not only is it folded incorrectly,

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.330
but as it gets into your brain,

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.303
it basically acts as a template

00:10:08.303 --> 00:10:10.711
for other proteins in your brain

00:10:10.711 --> 00:10:12.761
and causes them to misfold.

00:10:12.761 --> 00:10:14.990
And so as those proteins increasingly

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.260
are misfolded in your brain,

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:20.350
it causes little sections
of your brain to die.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:24.500
So it causes what's called
a spongiform encephalopathy.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.120
So that's the official
name for mad cow disease,

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.950
is bovine spongiform encephalopathy,

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:31.820
and it basically leads your brain

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:33.530
to become Swiss cheese over time.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.138
It can take decades,

00:10:35.138 --> 00:10:37.540
so the timeframe is pretty off here.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:39.294
Coming back from the dead is, as well.

00:10:39.294 --> 00:10:42.690
[Columbus screaming]
[zombies growling]

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.320
It can definitely cause
behavioral changes,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.530
you know, aggression or things like that.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.907
Although to the level of chasing people

00:10:48.907 --> 00:10:49.840
and trying to eat them,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.260
I don't think that's ever been established

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.912
with any kind of prion disease.

00:10:54.912 --> 00:10:56.912
I would give this one also maybe a five.

00:10:57.758 --> 00:10:58.608
Firefighter: We can't help you.

00:10:58.608 --> 00:11:00.600
We can't help you unless you calm down.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.030
An ambulance is on its way

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:03.951
right now, all right?
Woman: No! [screams]

00:11:03.951 --> 00:11:05.730
[medic screaming]
[blood squelching]

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:07.470
Smith: Yeah, so this is
kind of similar to, again,

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:08.910
that rabies aspect of it.

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.000
Rabies is probably the only
other virus that we know

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.080
that is basically 100% fatal.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:17.920
There have been a couple of
people who were infected.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.010
You can get what's called
postexposure prophylaxis.

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:25.540
So if you have not gotten
the rabies vaccine before

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:27.031
and you've been exposed,

00:11:27.031 --> 00:11:29.760
you've had a bite from a rabid dog

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.760
or a wild animal or exposed to a bat,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:37.190
you can get the rabies vaccine
shortly after your exposure.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.990
That can basically stop the virus

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:40.500
from replicating in your body,

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.380
so that will protect you from infection.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.410
But rabies has a very
long incubation period,

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:49.120
you know, weeks to
sometimes months or more.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.980
So once you have symptoms of rabies,

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.334
basically it is completely 100% fatal.

00:11:54.334 --> 00:11:56.813
Police: You need to
remain in the building!

00:11:56.813 --> 00:11:58.810
Smith: I mean, you would have
to get public health involved

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:00.360
really quickly, 'cause
they're the ones really

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.180
with the authority to shut anything down.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.750
So, presumably somebody would call 911,

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.600
call police, call an ambulance,

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:10.980
and it would go from the
hospital first responders

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:12.927
to the public-health
department, who would say,

00:12:12.927 --> 00:12:15.110
"OK, we have some kind
of an outbreak here."

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.880
We would quarantine everyone.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:18.740
So that would be a pretty rapid response.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.065
Yeah, I would give this, again,

00:12:20.065 --> 00:12:21.490
maybe, like, a three.

00:12:24.192 --> 00:12:27.775
[stroller wheels creaking]

00:12:30.872 --> 00:12:32.973
[zombie growls]

00:12:32.973 --> 00:12:34.129
[gun bangs]

00:12:34.129 --> 00:12:34.962
[zombie grunts]

00:12:34.962 --> 00:12:37.720
There are some fungal infections
that can affect the brain,

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:39.290
but they don't seem to cause

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:41.260
the type of behavioral changes like this.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:44.020
Dr. Caroline: The next stage
in the fungus' life cycle,

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:45.773
the mature sexual stage.

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.630
This is a sporangium, a seed pod.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.600
If they were to open...

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.050
Sgt. Eddie: What?

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.160
Dr. Caroline: The end of the world.

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:57.790
Smith: You know, this is
modeled on insect pathogens,

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:01.430
where the fungus infects the insect,

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.080
and it does change its behavior.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.080
They basically force the insects

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:08.440
to go as high as they can up a tree,

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:12.580
and eventually, the
fungus causes the insect

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.080
to just basically be a host

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.740
for kind of that next phase of evolution.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.060
They talk about it in there.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.110
But basically you have this fruiting body

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:23.180
that will replicate in the insect

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.480
and then kind of pop out
somewhere of their body,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.510
from their head or elsewhere.

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.380
And then that is what sporulates.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:31.150
And so, when it sporulates,

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.300
it releases all of the new fungi,

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:34.841
and then they kind of rain down

00:13:34.841 --> 00:13:37.232
over the colony of insects

00:13:37.232 --> 00:13:38.570
and can infect all of those.

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:40.820
So that's how it ends up amplifying.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.430
We have had fungal infections

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:45.640
that have gone around the
world or around a country.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:47.720
So, I live in an area in northeast Ohio

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.970
that used to be covered by chestnuts,

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:51.760
but then they all got a fungal disease

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.010
and they basically all died.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:55.782
This can happen. Those can be transported

00:13:55.782 --> 00:13:57.640
very easily through the air.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:59.520
So that's kind of the premise of this,

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:02.920
that if something was adapted to humans

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.920
that was like this cordyceps
that infects insects,

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:07.700
that this could be the result.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:08.770
I believe in The Last of Us

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:11.120
they have a lot of
different kind of phenotypes

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.170
or different types of zombies

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.860
that kind of evolve over
the course of infection.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:17.910
Yeah, I would say,

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.301
I would give this a six.

00:14:20.906 --> 00:14:22.081
Prisoner: No!
Guard: They don't look

00:14:22.081 --> 00:14:23.250
like volunteers to me.

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:24.900
Scientist: Millions of people
are dying for no reason.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.555
These men are gonna die
to help us find a vaccine.

00:14:27.555 --> 00:14:28.710
Smith: So, I love this show.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:30.760
I think "Z Nation" is
completely underrated

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.750
as far as zombie shows go.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:35.980
So, some things that are at
least somewhat accurate here

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:37.940
is that we do have a long history, one,

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.910
of doing experiments on prisoners.

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:42.260
For many years, if you
needed something done,

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.830
you went to prisons and
you went to orphanages,

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:46.670
because they were places

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.260
where you could get consent rather easily.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:51.750
Whether this is being done legally or not,

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:53.010
it would be actually very difficult

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:55.190
to do these experiments
on prisoners right now

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:57.060
because there's so many issues of consent.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.770
So that is actually kind of accurate,

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.570
although hopefully not anymore.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:03.910
So, you have Murphy, who was
injected with this serum.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:06.740
Of course, he gets
bitten and doesn't turn.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:08.163
So, throughout the show,

00:15:08.163 --> 00:15:10.960
he's kind of not only
resistant to the virus

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:12.660
but able to control other zombies,

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.030
which, again, may not be very realistic.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:16.710
But you do have people with perhaps

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:19.494
kind of partial immunity to
some of these infections.

00:15:19.494 --> 00:15:21.038
Murphy: Eight times

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:23.715
I was bitten,

00:15:24.581 --> 00:15:26.272
and did not turn!

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:32.292
Eight times I was infected
by their bloody saliva!

00:15:32.292 --> 00:15:35.140
Smith: Sometimes, vaccine
protection is complete.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:36.620
Sometimes it's incomplete.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:39.470
That's actually what we are expecting

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:40.940
for the coronavirus vaccine,

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.840
that we don't expect it
will be 100% effective.

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.220
But this is kind of what we
see for the influenza vaccine,

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.520
that it protects people from infection,

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.150
but not all of the time.

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:54.360
But even for people who are
vaccinated, get influenza,

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:56.170
you tend to have more mild symptoms,

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:59.010
so kind of what Murphy is seeing here.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:00.452
Yeah, probably about a two.

00:16:00.452 --> 00:16:02.727
It's pretty unrealistic,
especially some of the

00:16:02.727 --> 00:16:04.595
scenarios they get into later on.

00:16:04.595 --> 00:16:06.900
[dramatic music]
[water splashing]

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:10.130
[man yells]

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:12.118
David: It was a big plane, Russ.

00:16:12.118 --> 00:16:13.580
Russ: Why's that?

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:15.471
David: 'Cause we're right on top of it.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:21.760
Smith: If you're transporting
a very deadly bioweapon,

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:24.690
it would likely be in very tight storage,

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.900
multiple layers probably,
and you'd also have dry ice

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:29.570
or something to keep it cold.

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:32.970
So exactly how it was released
during this plane crash

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:34.970
is not very clear, I think.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.520
That water supply, the
virus would be diluted,

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.730
and even further so as it got spread out

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.460
to all the people of the town.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:44.100
So you'd have to have a really small

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:45.491
infectious dose for this,

00:16:45.491 --> 00:16:49.600
like one virus for people to get infected.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:51.133
And then, most likely the virus

00:16:51.133 --> 00:16:53.365
would be killed off in the water,

00:16:53.365 --> 00:16:56.560
because rabies is a virus
that needs a host to amplify.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:58.750
It's not typically a waterborne virus.

00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:01.120
So it's unclear exactly
how that would happen,

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:02.611
how it would survive in that water

00:17:02.611 --> 00:17:03.961
in order to infect those.

00:17:03.961 --> 00:17:05.500
If you don't have that knowledge

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.630
that a government plane went down

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:08.900
carrying a bioweapon there

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.690
and you're just doing this
based on the symptoms,

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:14.410
it would take a lot to
kind of tie those together.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:17.240
Usually when we see outbreaks
of waterborne disease,

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.880
we respond to them,
because you have upticks

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:23.490
in people reporting to their doctors

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.506
with gastrointestinal symptoms,

00:17:25.506 --> 00:17:28.064
with diarrhea and vomiting.

00:17:28.064 --> 00:17:30.718
One of the biggest-ever outbreaks

00:17:30.718 --> 00:17:35.040
of waterborne disease in the
United States in modern times

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.580
was in Milwaukee in 1993.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:41.290
They had an outbreak of a
parasite called cryptosporidium.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.407
And one of the early indicators for that

00:17:43.407 --> 00:17:47.030
was that all of the Imodium
was gone from stores.

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:48.840
People weren't necessarily
reporting to their doctor

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:50.650
that they had diarrhea,
but they were going out

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:52.450
for anti-diarrheal medicine.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:54.782
I'll put this down at
about a two. [laughs]

00:17:54.782 --> 00:17:56.145
[Kay screaming]
Andy: Oh, no. [grumbling]

00:17:56.145 --> 00:17:58.409
[Andy groaning]
[bite squelching]

00:17:58.409 --> 00:18:00.533
[Andy screams]

00:18:00.533 --> 00:18:03.001
Smith: While he's in
that incubation period,

00:18:03.001 --> 00:18:05.508
know that he is going to turn eventually.

00:18:05.508 --> 00:18:09.380
And so this idea of bringing
back pathogens from history

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.700
as you're digging into the ground,

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:15.160
as the permafrost thaws,
can some of these pathogens

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:19.200
that have been locked in ice
cores for millennia or longer,

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:20.210
could those come back?

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:21.540
Yes, some of them definitely can.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:24.940
Anthrax has caused outbreaks
in some areas of Siberia.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.570
One thing is that anthrax is
a really hearty bacterium.

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:31.590
It forms spores, so it's really
stable in the environment.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:34.320
But again, we don't know
how long those would last.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:38.870
If it's decades or centuries
or longer, we're not sure.

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:41.910
But I do want to kind of
put a flip side to this,

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.410
is that when scientists
were trying to study

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:46.930
the 1918 flu virus, so the one that caused

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:49.450
the last big global pandemic,

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:51.770
they found some samples from this

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:53.930
that were frozen in permafrost,

00:18:53.930 --> 00:18:56.720
so one in Alaska and
one in northern Europe.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:00.182
In Alaska in the 1950s,
scientists went up there,

00:19:00.182 --> 00:19:01.920
dug up some of the people who had died

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:03.145
and who were, again,

00:19:03.145 --> 00:19:05.495
preserved in permafrost for many years,

00:19:05.495 --> 00:19:07.000
took samples of their lungs,

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.420
tried to grow them in the laboratory,

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:09.480
completely unsuccessful.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:11.350
There was no live virus there.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.010
Did the same thing with
some of the samples

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:14.710
from, again, northern Europe,

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.020
just about 15 years ago or so.

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:20.750
So we were able to get
viral RNA from those,

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:22.760
and that's actually one of
the reasons we know so much

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.850
about the sequence of the 1918 flu virus,

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:26.220
but could not grow it at all.

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:29.120
And so most viruses are more fragile

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:30.653
than, like, bacteria,

00:19:30.653 --> 00:19:33.650
so a viral outbreak would
probably be less likely

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:36.985
from something that has
been long put in the ground.

00:19:36.985 --> 00:19:38.050
[fire crackling]
[crickets chirping]

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.498
Cleverman: They're poisoning
this land, you know?

00:19:40.498 --> 00:19:42.940
This country's changing.

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:46.490
Sick, we all get sick.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.400
Smith: Interrupting nature,
that's a big problem.

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:51.590
In West Africa, we had the Ebola outbreak

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:54.100
a couple years ago, the really big one,

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:57.182
and part of that may have been

00:19:57.182 --> 00:19:59.940
from the more frequent
introductions of the virus

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:03.230
into the human population
across parts of Africa

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:05.270
where they're being deforested.

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:06.990
Now the bats are all in close contact

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:08.380
and they're in closer contact

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:10.040
with other animals in the forest

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:12.170
because there's only this
little part of it left.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:14.872
So there's more potential for transmission

00:20:14.872 --> 00:20:17.422
for that virus to other animals.

00:20:17.422 --> 00:20:18.813
People eat those other animals.

00:20:18.813 --> 00:20:20.272
People come in contact with that

00:20:20.272 --> 00:20:22.700
through butchering them
or other types of things.

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:24.130
And so it can jump into humans.

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:26.640
So we see that cycle on and on.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:27.790
So maybe at a four.

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:31.415
I like "28 Days Later" just
'cause it really scares me.

00:20:31.415 --> 00:20:35.000
I can suspend disbelief
for all of the science

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:38.910
that's maybe exaggerated or
kind of mistold in there,

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:41.357
but the story just really freaks me out.

